\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7.2}{\ignorespaces Observed versus certified transients. Each point is a random reset state at a prime \(g<200\), run exactly (\(n=150\) seeds); the vertical axis is the observed number of carries after the first reset before the cycle is entered. The curves are the bounds \(H(g)\) (Theorem~\ref {thm:entry}) and \(K_{\mathrm  {poly}}(g)\) (Theorem~\ref {thm:Kpoly}). The largest observed index is 194 (\(g=197\)); the bounds exceed \(10^8\) there. They are worst-case guarantees, not predictions, and the gap between the points and the curves is Open Problem~\ref {op:exact}.}}{36}{figure.caption.14}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:bounds}{{7.2}{36}{Observed versus certified transients. Each point is a random reset state at a prime \(g<200\), run exactly (\(n=150\) seeds); the vertical axis is the observed number of carries after the first reset before the cycle is entered. The curves are the bounds \(H(g)\) (Theorem~\ref {thm:entry}) and \(K_{\mathrm {poly}}(g)\) (Theorem~\ref {thm:Kpoly}). The largest observed index is 194 (\(g=197\)); the bounds exceed \(10^8\) there. They are worst-case guarantees, not predictions, and the gap between the points and the curves is Open Problem~\ref {op:exact}}{figure.caption.14}{}}
